<title>BELGIUM: EU cuts U.S. approved poultry plant list.</title>
<headline>EU cuts U.S. approved poultry plant list.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-03-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>The list of European Union- approved poultry production factories in the United States has been cut by 30 because of unsatisfactory hygiene, an EU official said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>Farm ministers were told that troubled transatlantic negotiations to establish veterinary equivalency, allowing mutual recognition of standards, were in difficulty, the official added.</p>
<p>The  U.S. has said it will retaliate if an agreement is not in place by April 1 when new EU veterinary and sanitary border controls are due to come into force.</p>
<p>The new controls were to have come into force on January 1 but were postponed for three months to allow the negotiations to continue.</p>
<p>The controls would effectively block many U.S. products which do not meet the new standards.</p>
<p>Under threat in any reataliation by the U.S. would be Danish pigmeat exports worth $200,000 a year.</p>
<p>An EU negotiating team is due to visit Washington on March 24 and Farm Commissioner Franz Fischler told ministers that he is suggesting that the U.S. should agreee to raise their standards to EU levels within six months.</p>
</text>
